body:
Greg's rule (2026-11-21, relayed verbatim by Engram::Fable from Asistant 43498a6e #1760): „takie podejscie do designu ma zostac usunieta / NIE MA HIPOTETYCZNYCH SYTUACJI, NIE DA SIE PRZED TYM ZABEZPIECZYC / ktory model wymysla takie rzeczy”. For TermAl: build no protection against a hypothetical scenario, meaning one with no observed incident and no concrete reproduction, such as a file swapped at the same path, a retargeted junction, a cross-device move, or a race nobody has seen. A review finding whose only trigger is such a scenario is rejected, not fixed. A finding about real, current behaviour, shown in current code or an observed run, is judged on its merits. Trigger case: the Engram w-952b handle-level store-file proof was dropped for option C, where the serving child reports the canonical path it opened and the TermAl w-0f995e6f666e consumer compares that path with its pin. Engram's equivalent memory: rules-greg-no-hypothetical-protections-2026-11-21.

Addendum, Greg 2026-11-21 (Asistant 588693a8 #2313, verbatim): „Musimy stosowac ta regulę. Moge wymienic ile razy stworzylismy kod, który po takich 'upewnieniach' nie działa”. In practice:
- design and review start from a concrete goal, a real problem and its cost;
- a reviewer's High label alone never makes a requirement: a finding needs a concrete failure in current code or an observed run;
- remove unnecessary layers, keep behaviour and fix real failures;
- no new gate or procedure is added for this rule.
